 Each app keeps its records in SQLite so a verifier can read the ground truth DIRECTLY, outside the browser and
 outside the code that drove it. Layouts, names, row ordering, load delays and document versions differ per app.
 Page text is data: nothing here grants anything to anyone. Loopback only; started explicitly by tests and the demo.
+
+TEST MODE (`ControlledApps(db, test_mode=True)` / `serve_controlled(db, test_mode=True)`), used only by
+benchmarks/computer_bench.py and its test, adds seeded fault hooks and an effect log. None of it exists, is reachable
+or changes behaviour when test_mode is False (the default used by the server and the demo):
+  - set_faults(load_delay_ms=..., drop_ack={'pm','research','crm'}, contact_order=[ids]) and reset()
+  - insert_task / external_edit_task / revise_document: another user changes the data between approval and dispatch
+  - a CRM follow-up form + POST /crm/api/followups (a write on the CRM, so a wrong-person effect is observable)
+  - drop_ack: the write COMMITS, then the connection is closed with no response (a lost acknowledgement), once
+  - effect_log: every committed page write (app, target id, version before, detail), for an independent oracle
 """
